{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"01","section":"01","chapter_title":"Digital Citizenship and AI Readiness","title":"Digital Identity and Professional Presence","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting digital identity and professional presence to resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Convert a duty statement into an accomplishment statement built from a strong action verb plus a quantified, truthful result.","Tailor resume claims to a specific role so a reader sees fit in the top third of the page.","Disclose any AI editing help with a note that records what you accepted and what you rejected.","Keep every claim defensible: write nothing you could not back up if a reference checker asked."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness","cat100_supplement_digital_identity_and_professional_presence","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":1,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":[],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_1_1_digital_identity_and_professional_presence_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_1_1_digital_identity_and_professional_presence_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_1_1_digital_identity_and_professional_presence_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_1_1_digital_identity_and_professional_presence_calibration"]},"content":"# Digital Identity and Professional Presence\n\n![Digital Identity and Professional Presence textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/01-01-digital-identity-and-professional-presence.png)\n\n<youtube-embed id=\"FF83OevoW6U\" title=\"NACE Career Readiness Competencies\" caption=\"Watch after drafting your first profile claim. Use the competencies to decide which professional evidence belongs in the artifact and which details should stay out.\"></youtube-embed>\n\n**Watch for:** Which competency does your claim actually prove, and which detail would overreach?\n**Use this here because:** The video turns a vague profile sentence into a checkable professional claim, which is the same move this section asks you to make in the resume revision.\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nYour digital identity is the version of you that a stranger meets before you walk into the room. When a hiring manager searches your name, when a scholarship committee opens your LinkedIn, or when a recruiter scans the top third of your resume for roughly seven seconds (an average of 7.4 seconds in Ladders' 2018 eye-tracking study), they build a fast model of who you are from whatever evidence is visible. This section is about controlling that evidence on purpose instead of leaving it to chance.\n\nThe work product for this section is a resume before-after revision that carries truthful evidence, shows clear role fit, and includes an AI-use note. A resume is not a list of duties; it is a short argument that says, \"Hire me, and here is the proof.\" Each line you keep should earn its space by showing a result a reader could, in principle, verify with a manager, a transcript, or a record.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Convert a duty statement into an accomplishment statement built from a strong action verb plus a quantified, truthful result.\n- Tailor resume claims to a specific role so a reader sees fit in the top third of the page.\n- Disclose any AI editing help with a note that records what you accepted and what you rejected.\n- Keep every claim defensible: write nothing you could not back up if a reference checker asked.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nMaya is applying for a part-time front-desk job at the campus recreation center. Her draft resume says, \"Responsible for the front desk at the rec center.\" It is true, but it tells the reader nothing about whether she was good at it. A second applicant wrote, \"Processed about 60 member check-ins per shift with zero equipment losses across 14 shifts.\" Same job, very different signal. The second line gives the reader numbers to picture and a result to trust.\n\nThe difference is not confidence or fancy wording. It is evidence. Maya did the same work; she just had not yet translated it into a claim a reader could weigh.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA professional resume line is an accomplishment statement, and accomplishment statements follow a reliable shape: **action verb + task + quantified result**. The verb signals what you did (processed, designed, co-led), the task names the work, and the number turns a vague impression into a measurable outcome. This is why [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined) lists communication and professionalism as core competencies: employers are not just buying your time, they are buying your ability to make your contribution legible. The same move is a named digital-citizenship competency: the ISTE Standards for Students expect learners to \"cultivate and manage their digital identity and reputation\" and to stay aware of the permanence of their actions in the digital world (Standard 1.2 Digital Citizen, indicator 1.2.a).\n\nThree rules separate strong resume identity from weak resume identity.\n\n| Principle | Weak version | Strong version |\n|-----------|--------------|----------------|\n| Result over duty | \"Responsible for social media\" | \"Grew club Instagram from 80 to 240 followers in one semester\" |\n| Truthful and verifiable | \"Expert event planner\" | \"Co-led a 4-person team that ran one 60-person event\" |\n| Role fit in the top third | Generic objective line | A summary aimed at the exact posting's keywords |\n\nTruthfulness is not optional polish; it is the foundation. A resume claim is a promise that survives a reference check. \"Managed all operations\" when you staffed one desk is not an exaggeration, it is a claim that collapses the moment someone calls your supervisor. Role fit is the second discipline: the same true accomplishment can be ordered and worded to match a posting, because a reader spends most of their attention on the top third of the page.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A first-year applicant writes \\\"Spearheaded a company-wide initiative\\\" for organizing a 12-person study group. Which response best fits the accomplishment-statement rule?\" explanation=\"The verb-plus-number shape only works when the number and verb are truthful. A modest, verifiable claim survives a reference check; an inflated one is a red flag because the scope does not match the stage.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Keep it: bigger-sounding verbs always read as stronger\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Rewrite to a true, verifiable claim such as \\\"Organized a 12-person study group that met weekly for a semester\\\"\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Add more adjectives like \\\"dynamic\\\" and \\\"results-driven\\\"\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Remove all numbers so nothing can be checked\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nThe weak version below sounds plausible, but it does not yet give a reader enough evidence:\n\n> I made the resume bullet clearer.\n\nThat claim is too thin because \"clearer\" does not name the verb, the result, or the source you could verify. A stronger revision shows the actual line that changed and why the new wording is both more useful and still truthful.\n\n### Worked Example: revising one resume bullet, step by step\n\nTake a real before/after on a single bullet for the campus rec-center job.\n\n1. **Before (duty only):** `Responsible for the front desk at the rec center.`\n2. **Name the result you can verify.** You checked in members and logged equipment. A printout shows roughly 60 check-ins per shift and zero lost-equipment reports during your 14 shifts.\n3. **Pick an action verb + number.** Verb: `Processed`. Number: `60 member check-ins per shift`. Outcome: `zero equipment losses across 14 shifts`.\n4. **After (result, truthful, verifiable):** `Processed about 60 member check-ins per shift and tracked equipment with zero recorded losses across 14 shifts.`\n5. **AI-use note.** You asked AI to tighten the wording. It suggested `Managed all rec center operations` (false: you did not manage operations). You **reject** that line, log it as rejected, and keep the truthful version.\n\nResult: the bullet went from a duty with no evidence to one verb, two verifiable numbers, and a logged AI decision a reader can audit.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Predict each move as you revise the rec-center bullet\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Starting line: \\\"Responsible for the front desk at the rec center.\\\" What is the single biggest weakness a reviewer sees first?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"It is too short\",\"It states a duty but no verifiable result\",\"It uses the wrong font\",\"It mentions the rec center\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"It names a duty with no result. A reader cannot tell whether you were good at the job, only that you held it.\"},{\"prompt\":\"You have a printout showing about 60 check-ins per shift across 14 shifts with zero equipment losses. Which rewrite is both stronger AND truthful?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Managed all rec center operations\",\"Processed about 60 member check-ins per shift and tracked equipment with zero recorded losses across 14 shifts\",\"Expert front-desk professional\",\"Responsible for an extremely busy front desk\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"This version adds an action verb plus two verifiable numbers. Every figure could be confirmed by the printout, so it survives a reference check.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The AI editor suggests replacing your line with \\\"Managed all rec center operations.\\\" What do you log?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Accept it: it sounds more senior\",\"Reject it and log why: you staffed one desk, you did not manage operations\",\"Accept it silently\",\"Delete the AI note entirely\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"You reject and log it. A false claim collapses on a reference check, and the rejected-suggestion line is the evidence that your judgment, not the tool, controlled the wording.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Final result: one verb, two verifiable numbers, and a logged AI rejection. The duty became an accomplishment a reader can audit.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nTo lock the shape in, practice ordering the revision steps yourself.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Start from the duty line as written\",\"Name the result you can actually verify (check-ins, equipment record)\",\"Pick an action verb and attach the verifiable numbers\",\"Write the after line: verb plus task plus quantified result\",\"Log the AI suggestion you rejected and why\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nFor any resume line, run this test before you keep it: Can a reference checker confirm it, and does it help the specific role? If a manager you actually worked for would nod when read the line, it is defensible. If it helps a reader picture you succeeding in the posted job, it earns the top third of the page. A line that is true but irrelevant gets moved down or cut; a line that is impressive but unverifiable gets rewritten until it is honest.\n\n1. **Verb-and-number check:** Does the line start with an action verb and contain at least one concrete result?\n2. **Reference check:** Would your former supervisor confirm this exact wording without flinching?\n3. **Fit check:** Does this line match a real requirement in the posting you are targeting?\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common mistake is inflating a claim to sound senior. \"Spearheaded a company-wide initiative\" reads as a red flag on a freshman resume because the reader knows the scope does not match the stage. A smaller, true claim (\"organized a 12-person study group that met weekly for a semester\") is stronger precisely because it is believable.\n\nA second misreading is treating the AI editor as a co-author whose suggestions are facts. AI can tighten phrasing, but it does not know what you actually did. When it invents a number or a title, accepting it silently turns your resume into fiction. The disclosure note exists so that your judgment, not the tool's guess, stays the source of truth.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nImagine you are converting an entire \"Experience\" section, not just one line. Start by listing every duty you can remember from a job or activity. Next to each duty, write the result: what changed because you were there? If you cannot find a result, ask whether the duty belongs on the resume at all. Then attach a number wherever one honestly exists: how many people, how often, how much, how long. Numbers do not have to be large; \"tutored 3 students twice a week for 10 weeks\" is more persuasive than \"tutored students\" because it is concrete.\n\nOnce the lines carry results, reorder them for the target role. If you are applying to a data-entry job, the check-in throughput line goes first; if you are applying to a leadership role, the co-led-a-team line leads. The same true facts produce two different resumes because role fit decides the order. Finally, add the AI-use note as a short line in your submission record, not on the resume itself, so an instructor or employer can see that the wording is yours even where a tool helped you polish it.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read other people's resumes, practice spotting which lines would survive a reference check and which would not. Keep a two-column note: on the left, the claim as written; on the right, the evidence that would have to exist for it to be true. A line like \"increased revenue by 30%\" demands a baseline, a final figure, and a time window. If those are missing, the claim is decoration, not evidence.\n\nA strong reader of professional presence can answer four questions about any line:\n\n1. What did this person actually do, in plain language?\n2. What result proves it mattered?\n3. Could that result be confirmed by a third party?\n4. Does the line fit the job being applied for?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour before-after revision is strong when a future employer can read the \"after\" line and predict how you would perform in the role without needing you to explain. Treat formatting (fonts, spacing, bullet symbols) as the last layer. The earlier layers are the verb, the result, the truthfulness, and the role fit. If a line lacks a result, mark it and ask BigAL to help you find the measurable outcome hidden inside the duty rather than asking it to make the line \"sound better.\"\n\nA useful note for your submission would read: \"I rewrote the rec-center bullet from a duty into a result with two verifiable numbers. The AI editor suggested an inflated 'managed all operations' line; I rejected it because I could not back it up in a reference check, and a false claim is worse than a modest one.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nNow move the same skill to a different surface: your LinkedIn headline and your email signature. A resume bullet, a LinkedIn \"About\" sentence, and a signature line are all the same identity problem under different space limits. On LinkedIn you have room for a sentence of context, so the rec-center accomplishment might read, \"Reliable front-desk operator who kept equipment loss at zero across a busy semester.\" In an email signature you have only a phrase, so it shrinks to \"CAT 100 student | campus rec-center staff.\" The truthful core never changes; the constraint (length, audience, formality) decides how much of the evidence you show. If you understand identity as evidence rather than as decoration, you can re-cut it for any surface without lying to fit the space.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/01-01-digital-identity-and-professional-presence-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"01.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting duty line. The revised version should show the accomplishment statement with a verb and a verifiable number. The trace should explain why the change is truthful and why at least one AI suggestion was rejected.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak duty line and a strong accomplishment line side by side.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same verb-plus-number rule to a new role or posting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the order of your bullets.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising a resume bullet for CAT 100 01.01. A peer comment says one line sounds inflated for a first-year applicant and may not survive a reference check. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would make the line both truthful and role-relevant.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_digital_identity_and_professional_presence\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\", \"cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_digital_identity_and_professional_presence\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that your resume revision records the duty you started from, the verb and number you added, the role you tailored it for, and the AI suggestion you rejected and why.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- Ladders, Inc. (2018). Recruiter eye-tracking study (7.4-second average initial resume screen). https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/ladders-updates-popular-recruiter-eye-tracking-study-with-new-key-insights-on-how-job-seekers-can-improve-their-resumes-300744217.html\n- ISTE Standards for Students, 1.2 Digital Citizen (indicator 1.2.a, digital identity). https://iste.org/standards/students\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_1_digital_identity_and_professional_presence_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_digital_identity_and_professional_presence","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_digital_identity_and_professional_presence","cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness"],"stem":"A student writes the resume bullet: \"Responsible for the social media of the robotics club.\" Which revision turns it into employer-ready evidence?","options":["Helped out with the robotics club's social media accounts.","Grew the robotics club Instagram from 80 to 240 followers in one semester by posting a weekly build update.","Passionate and detail-oriented social media manager for robotics.","Responsible for managing all robotics club online presence and branding."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A strong bullet pairs an action verb with a measurable, verifiable result (80 to 240 followers, one semester). The others restate the duty or add adjectives without evidence.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_1_digital_identity_and_professional_presence_duties_not_results","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_1_digital_identity_and_professional_presence_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Your resume lists \"Expert in Python\" but you have only finished one intro CAT 100 module that used short scripts. What is the most defensible fix?","options":["Leave it as \"Expert in Python\" because it sounds stronger to recruiters.","Change it to \"Familiar with Python (intro coursework: wrote short data-cleaning scripts in CAT 100).\"","Delete every mention of Python so nothing can be questioned.","Change it to \"Advanced Python developer with production experience.\""],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Truthful evidence means the claim matches what you can show. Naming the level (familiar) and the source (intro coursework) is defensible; inflating to expert or advanced is not.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_1_digital_identity_and_professional_presence_inflate_truth","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_1_digital_identity_and_professional_presence_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"You ask an AI tool to tighten your resume bullets. It rewrites one as \"Led a 12-person engineering team.\" You were one of 12 members, not the lead. What belongs in your AI-use note and final resume?","options":["Accept the bullet as written; the AI is usually correct about phrasing.","Reject that suggestion, record it as rejected in the AI-use note, and keep a truthful bullet such as \"Contributed as one of 12 members of the engineering build team.\"","Keep the AI bullet but add an emoji so it looks original.","Remove all AI involvement and do not mention that AI helped at all."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI is a bounded helper. You must reject claims that are untrue, log the decision in the AI-use note, and keep the surviving bullet verifiable.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_1_digital_identity_and_professional_presence_ai_undisclosed","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_1_digital_identity_and_professional_presence_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"You are applying to a data-entry internship that lists \"Excel\" and \"attention to detail\" as top requirements. Your resume leads with a graphic-design club bullet. What is the best role-fit revision?","options":["Send the same resume; reordering is not worth the effort.","Move your Excel and data-accuracy bullets to the top so the most role-relevant evidence appears first, then keep design lower.","Delete every bullet that is not about Excel.","Add the word \"Excel\" five times so the keyword scanner ranks you higher."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Role fit means ordering and keywords match the posting. Lead with the most relevant verifiable evidence; do not delete real experience or keyword-stuff.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_1_digital_identity_and_professional_presence_one_resume_fits_all","difficulty":"medium"}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_1_digital_identity_and_professional_presence_duties_not_results","pattern":"resume_bullet_lists_duties_not_results","description":"Writing resume bullets that list job duties or responsibilities instead of measurable results an employer can verify","trigger":"bullet starts with 'Responsible for' or names a task with no number, scope, or outcome","feedback":"Rewrite the bullet as action verb plus measurable result. 'Responsible for social media' becomes 'Grew club Instagram from 80 to 240 followers in one semester.'","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_1_digital_identity_and_professional_presence_inflate_truth","pattern":"inflating_or_inventing_evidence","description":"Adding skills, titles, or numbers the student cannot back up because they sound impressive","trigger":"learner lists a tool, title, or metric with no artifact, course, or experience behind it","feedback":"Every resume claim must be truthful and defensible. Keep only what you can show with coursework, a project, or a verifiable record.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_1_digital_identity_and_professional_presence_ai_undisclosed","pattern":"ai_edits_treated_as_authorship","description":"Treating AI wording suggestions as if the student wrote them, with no note of what AI changed","trigger":"learner pastes AI-rewritten bullets without recording which suggestions were accepted, modified, or rejected","feedback":"Keep an AI-use note: list which AI suggestions you accepted, which you changed, and which you rejected, and confirm each surviving claim is still true.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_1_digital_identity_and_professional_presence_one_resume_fits_all","pattern":"single_resume_ignores_role_fit","description":"Sending the same resume to every role without matching bullets to the posting's required skills","trigger":"learner submits one fixed resume across different job postings","feedback":"Tailor the bullet order and keywords to the specific posting so the most role-relevant evidence appears first.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"a6fe1cf916fef7a4d8e9b0b2d4aee07719268da377968e841a35c6aab7b08dfe","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}